#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""spacequilt - a modern take on xdiskusage's icicle disk-usage view.

Scans a directory with `du` (exactly the trick the original xdiskusage uses)
and draws the result as horizontal, size-proportional columns. Click a block
to drill into it; use the breadcrumb or Backspace to go back out.

Usage:
    spacequilt [PATH] [-X/--cross-filesystems] [--colors SCHEME]

  PATH               Directory to scan. Default: current directory.
  -X, --cross-filesystems  Descend into other mounted filesystems too. By
                     default du is run with -x, so scanning / measures just
                     the root filesystem instead of every mount under it.
                     Scanning a mount point also shows the volume's free
                     space as a block next to the scanned directory.
  --colors SCHEME    classic (hue per top-level child, lighter with depth),
                     spacemonger (fixed palette cycling by depth), or rainbow
                     (every level re-spreads the hues, first child keeps the
                     parent's). Remembered between runs; the toolbar has the
                     same choice.
  --style STYLE      bevel (default) or flat. bevel: blocks get a light top/left
                     edge, a dark bottom/right edge and a soft gradient, a
                     nod to SpaceMonger's raised tiles. Remembered too; the
                     toolbar's 3D toggle is the same switch.

Interaction:
  Wheel             Zoom continuously around the cursor.
  Shift / Ctrl + wheel   Zoom one axis only (sizes / depth).
  Drag              Pan the view.
  Left click        Select the block under the cursor.
  Double click      Drill into it (double click the focused column to go up).
  0 / Ctrl+0        Fit the view back to the focused node.
  Breadcrumb / Backspace   Go up one level.
  Right click       Context menu (open in file manager, copy path).
  Ctrl+R            Rescan the current root.
  Ctrl+O            Pick a new directory to scan.
  Ctrl+M            Toggle staying on one filesystem.
  Esc               Cancel a running scan, otherwise go up one level.
"""

